{"id":"0a8281a5-e363-4354-aab7-766a81f24ef5","object":"chat.completion","created":1785032465,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    id_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    retained = set()\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\\n            cur = n['id']\\n            while cur is not None:\\n                retained.add(cur)\\n                cur = id_map[cur].get('parent')\\n    removed_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] not in retained])\\n    retained_ids = sorted([n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] in retained])\\n    return {\\\\\\\"removed_node_ids\\\\\\\": removed_ids, \\\\\\\"retained_node_ids\\\\\\\": retained_ids}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to write a solve function that takes a case payload (as shown in the fixtures) and returns {\"removed_node_ids\": [...], \"retained_node_ids\": [...]}. The task is about removing nodes from a tree while preserving required symbols. Looking at the payload: nodes with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Required symbols list. We need to decide which nodes to remove (removable nodes) such that all required symbols are still present in the retained nodes, and likely maximizing total utility? Or some criterion? The problem statement doesn't specify exactly, but the examples in the fixtures suggest a specific algorithm. This is a typical \"tree pruning with required nodes\" problem. Probably we need to keep the root and all nodes that are ancestors of required symbols, and also keep the required symbols themselves. Removable nodes that are not needed for any required symbol can be removed. But also note that removable nodes might be kept if they are ancestors of required symbols? Actually, the tree structure: nodes have parent references. The required symbols are strings like \"root\", \"s1\". We need to retain nodes that have those symbols, and also all ancestors of those nodes (since ancestors are needed to reach them). But ancestors might be removable? In the data, root is not removable. Some ancestors might be removable, but we cannot remove them if they are needed to retain a required symbol. So algorithm:\n\n- Build a mapping from id to node.\n- Build children mapping.\n- Identify nodes that have symbols in required_symbols. These are \"required nodes\".\n- For each required node, traverse up to root and mark all ancestors as \"retained\".\n- Also mark the required nodes themselves as retained.\n- Any node not marked retained is eligible for removal if it is removable. But we might also consider that some removable nodes could be retained if they are ancestors of other retained nodes? Already covered by ancestor marking.\n- However, what about nodes that are not ancestors but are still part of the tree? They can be removed if removable.\n- But note: there might be nodes that are removable but are ancestors of multiple required nodes? Already marked.\n- Also, there might be nodes that are not required but are ancestors of removable nodes that are kept? Only required nodes matter.\n- So final retained set: all nodes that are either required or ancestors of required (including root). Then removed nodes are those that are removable and not in retained set.\n- Also need to consider that some removable nodes might be retained even if not required? No, the goal is to remove as many as possible while keeping required symbols. So only keep those necessary.\n- However, check the fixtures: let's test with first case.\n\nCase 0 payload:\n\nnodes:\nroot (depth0, removable false)\nn1 (parent root, symbol s1, depth1, removable false) – has required symbol s1, so retained.\nn2 (parent root, symbol s2, depth1, removable true) – not required, ancestors of required? n2 is not ancestor of any required node (required at n1 and root). So n2 can be removed if removable? But n2 has children n4. n4 has symbol s1, required. So n4 is child of n2. n2 is ancestor of n4, so n2 must be retained because n4 is required. So n2 is ancestor of required, so retained even though removable.\nn3 (parent root, symbol s0, depth1, removable false) – not required, no required descendants? n3 child n5 has symbol s2, not required. So n3 is not needed, but not removable, so it remains anyway.\nn4 (parent n2, symbol s1, depth2, removable true) – has required s1, so retained.\nn5 (parent n3, symbol s2, depth2, removable false) – not required, not ancestor, not removable.\nn6 (parent n1, symbol s0, depth2, removable true) – not required, but is it ancestor of any required? n1 is retained, n6 is child of n1, but n6 does not have required symbol. Is n6 ancestor of any required? No. So n6 can be removed.\n\nSo retained: root, n1, n2, n3 (non-removable), n4, n5 (non-removable). n6 is removable and not required, so removed. But n2 is retained even though removable because it is ancestor of n4. So removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"]. retained_node_ids = [\"root\",\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\"].\n\nNow check desired output? Not provided. But likely that's the algorithm.\n\nWhat about the \"utility\" field? Maybe we need to consider maximizing utility? But the problem statement doesn't mention utility. The fixtures have utility values, but they might be used for tie-breaking? However, the given data seems to have only one solution set. The problem might be to remove all removable nodes that are not essential for the required symbols. So we just need to find essential nodes.\n\nLet's verify with case 1 (b-001):\n\nnodes:\nroot (removable false)\nn1 (parent root, s1, removable false) -> retained (required)\nn2 (parent root, s2, removable true) -> ancestor? n4 (s1) is child of n2, so n2 must be retained\nn3 (parent root, s0, removable false) -> not required, child n5 (s2) not required, so retained because non-removable\nn4 (parent n2, s1, removable true) -> required, retained\nn5 (parent n3, s2, removable false) -> not required, not ancestor, non-removable so retained\nn6 (parent n1, s0, removable true) -> not required, not ancestor, so removable and removed\n\nSo again removed: n6.\n\nCase 2 (b-002): same pattern. n6 removable, not required, not ancestor. So removed: n6.\n\nThus the algorithm seems consistent.\n\nBut need to consider when a removable node is not an ancestor of any required node but its subtree contains a required node? Actually if subtree contains required, then it is ancestor. So that's covered.\n\nEdge cases: multiple required symbols at different depths. Also, nodes may have multiple children. The ancestor traversal covers all.\n\nThus solve function:\n\n- Input case: dict with keys \"nodes\" (list of dicts) and \"required_symbols\" (list of strings), and maybe \"_case_nonce\". Ignore nonce.\n- Build dict id_to_node.\n- Build parent-child adjacency.\n- Find set of required_node_ids: nodes where symbol in required_symbols.\n- For each required node id, traverse up (using parent) until root, adding all visited ids to retained_ids set. Also add the required node itself.\n- Also ensure root is included (since root has null parent and is required by required_symbols? root symbol is \"root\" which is in required_symbols, so root will be included anyway.\n- After marking, all retained_ids are those essential.\n- Then removed_node_ids = [node.id for node in nodes if node.removable and node.id not in retained_ids]\n- retained_node_ids = [node.id for node in nodes if node.id in retained_ids] (but we already have set, just convert to list).\n- Sort? The output schema doesn't specify order, but likely the order from the original list might be fine. Could sort for determinism. I'll sort by id (strings).\n- Return dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nOne more nuance: What about a removable node that is an ancestor of a required node but itself is not required and maybe its parent is also removable? The algorithm already includes it via ancestor traversal.\n\nPotential issue: If a required node is removable? In data, required symbols include \"root\" and \"s1\". Nodes with s1 are removable in some cases (n4). But they are required, so they must be retained. That's fine.\n\nImplementation details: use recursion or loop to traverse parent chain. Ensure we handle root's parent null.\n\nNow write solve function. Must return JSON-serializable dict.\n\nImplementation code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    id_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    # Build parent mapping\n    # Mark retained\n    retained_ids = set()\n    for node in nodes:\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\n            # traverse up\n            cur = node['id']\n            while cur is not None:\n                retained_ids.add(cur)\n                parent = id_map[cur].get('parent')\n                cur = parent\n    # Also ensure root? root already covered.\n    removed = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['removable'] and n['id'] not in retained_ids]\n    retained = [n['id'] for n in nodes if n['id'] in retained_ids]\n    # optional sort\n    removed.sort()\n    retained.sort()\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained}\n\nEdge: The parent field might be None or null. In JSON, null maps to None. So check.\n\nNow produce the implementation as a JSON string.\n\nWe need to output a JSON object with key \"implementation\", value being a string of Python source code. Must escape newlines, backslashes, quotes.\n\nLet's write the code as a string.\n\nCode:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    id_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    retained = set()\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n['symbol'] in required:\n            cur = n['id']\n            while cur is not None:\n                retained.add(cur)\n                cu